.Dd September 3, 2026
.Dt ZFS_REBASE 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm zfs_rebase
.Nd rebase one ZFS filesystem onto another
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl n
.Op Fl p
.Op Fl v
.Op Fl o Ar file
.Op Fl -verify
.Op Fl -overwrite
.Oo Fl -allow-unrelated Oo Fl -base Ar snapshot Oc Oc
.Fl -from Ar snapshot Ns | Ns Ar dataset
.Fl -onto Ar snapshot Ns | Ns Ar dataset
.Fl -result Ar name
.Nm
.Fl -continue
.Op Fl -verify
.Op Fl v
.Fl -result Ar dataset
.Nm
.Fl -restart
.Op Fl v
.Fl -result Ar dataset
.Nm
.Fl -abort
.Op Fl v
.Fl -result Ar dataset
.Nm
.Fl -verify
.Op Fl v
.Fl -result Ar dataset
.Nm
.Fl -posix
.Op Fl p
.Op Fl o Ar file
.Ar basedir fromdir ontodir
.Nm
.Fl -build-fixture Ar fixture dir
.Nm
.Fl -edit-fixture Ar fixture tree dir
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility rebases one ZFS filesystem onto another.
Given two sides that diverged from a common base, it replays the changes of
one side onto the other, or reports the names it could not decide and why.
It is not a
.Xr zfs 8
verb and it is not built into the kernel: it is a userspace program that
does all of its work through the
.Sy libzfs_core
and
.Sy libzfs
libraries, execs nothing, and must run as root.
.Pp
Three trees are read, each through the
.Pa .zfs/snapshot
directory of its dataset:
.Ar base ,
the branch point;
.Ar from ,
the side whose changes are replayed; and
.Ar onto ,
the side they are replayed onto.
Only the two sides are named.
The base is derived: each side's origin chain is walked back \(em the
snapshot, then its dataset's origin, then that dataset's origin \(em to the
nearest dataset both descend from, and the older of the two snapshots they
name there, by
.Sy createtxg ,
is the base.
Two sides that share no origin are refused unless
.Fl -allow-unrelated
is given, and so is a pair whose derived base turns out to be one of the
arguments: one side already contains the other, and what is wanted there is
not a rebase.
.Pp
The decision is made over names, over hardlink pools \(em a pool being one
file and every name it has \(em and over content.
A name only one side touched takes that side's version; a pool edited
through one of its names is edited for every name of it; a name both sides
changed differently, a pool the two sides disagree about, a split one side
healed and an addition whose every anchor the other side deleted are
conflicts, and a conflict stops the names it covers and nothing else.
.Pp
The one output is a manifest.
It is a single document in two sections: a scoped walk of the result
namespace, in which every name with something to say carries an action \(em
.Cm rm ,
.Cm ln Ar path ,
.Cm cp Ar path ,
.Cm dup Ar path ,
.Cm write Ar path
or
.Cm conflict Ar n
\(em and, after it, one record per conflict giving its class, a one-sentence
why, and the pools of that group in each of the three trees.
A header names the three snapshots the run actually read, the mode, and the
counts of actions and conflicts.
Names are escaped as a backslash and three octal digits wherever they are
not plain printable ASCII.
The format is a document of its own,
.Pa v4-manifest.md
in the project's notes, and it is the contract between this tool and
whatever applies or answers it.
.Pp
The manifest is then applied.
.Fl -onto
decides where:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Sy The clone form ,
.Fl -onto
given a snapshot.
.Fl -result
names a new dataset, created as a read-only clone of that snapshot at the
run's own private mountpoint, and the rebase is made in it.
Nothing of yours is written to at all.
.It
.Sy The dataset form ,
.Fl -onto
given a dataset.
The rebase is made in that dataset itself, and
.Fl -result
names the snapshot the tool takes of it before anything is applied, which
is kept afterwards as the before-image.
Exclusivity is the unmount: the dataset is unmounted from its own
mountpoint and mounted at the run's private directory instead, with its
.Sy mountpoint
property untouched.
A dataset somebody is using will not unmount, and then the tool says so and
exits 2.
Nothing is ever forced.
Wherever the run stops \(em at conflicts, at done, at a failure, at a signal
\(em the dataset is handed back: off the private mount, readonly as it was,
mounted where its
.Sy mountpoint
property says.
.El
.Pp
A side given as a dataset rather than a snapshot is snapshotted by the tool
under a generated name, recorded as tool-made, and destroyed when the
rebase ends, at done or at
.Fl -abort .
If that snapshot were wanted, a snapshot would have been passed.
.Pp
A rebase outlives the process that started it.
What makes it one thing rather than a process's leavings is the record it
writes on the result, the holds it takes on its inputs, and the gates it
passes; see
.Sx THE RECORD ,
.Sx HOLDS
and
.Sx THE GATES .
.Sh OPTIONS
The inputs and the form of the run:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl -from Ar snapshot Ns | Ns Ar dataset
The side whose changes are replayed.
Spelled
.Fl -off-of
as well.
A dataset here is snapshotted by the tool.
.It Fl -onto Ar snapshot Ns | Ns Ar dataset
The side they are replayed onto, and the form of the run: a snapshot is
cloned as
.Fl -result ,
a dataset is rebased in place.
.It Fl -result Ar name
Two meanings, one per form.
With
.Fl -onto
a snapshot it is the dataset the rebased clone is created as.
With
.Fl -onto
a dataset it is the name of the pre-apply snapshot taken of that dataset \(em
the short name after the
.Sy @ ,
or a full name whose dataset part is onto.
For every verb it is the dataset carrying the record, and a snapshot name
is taken as its dataset, so both spellings find the same rebase.
.It Fl -allow-unrelated
Rebase two sides that share no origin.
The base is not derived; it is
.Fl -base
or the empty tree.
Pruning is turned off with it, because object numbers mean nothing across
lineages.
.It Fl -base Ar snapshot
Only with
.Fl -allow-unrelated :
the tree to treat as the branch point.
Its
.Sy createtxg
must be at or below that of both inputs.
.It Fl -overwrite
In the dataset form, replace a record whose rebase reached done.
An open rebase is never overwritten by any flag:
.Fl -continue
or
.Fl -abort
settles it first.
The clone form never overwrites anything, since every result is a fresh
dataset with a fresh record.
.El
.Pp
The flags:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl n
Dry run.
The manifest is written and nothing else: no clone, no record, no holds,
and
.Fl -result
is ignored.
Where a side was given as a dataset the tool still snapshots it, because
there has to be something to read, and destroys that snapshot again before
it exits.
.It Fl p
Permissive-merge mode.
Sharing that strict mode raises as an
.Sy unexpressed-sharing
conflict is resolved instead.
.It Fl v
Report counts and the steps taken on standard error.
.It Fl o Ar file
Write the manifest to
.Ar file
rather than into the run directory.
The file is yours: the tool records the path and does not remove it, where
its own manifest goes with the run.
.It Fl -verify
Asked for at the start of a run, or with
.Fl -continue ,
this is the flag: the final check is made once, after the last stage
verified and before anything is released, and the request is written into
the record so a kill and a
.Fl -continue
still honour it.
With
.Fl -continue
the check is also a repair.
Alone on a result it is a verb; see below.
.El
.Pp
The verbs work on a rebase that already exists.
Each takes
.Fl -result
and
.Fl v
and nothing else, no two of them go together, and every one keys on the
record and on nothing else: a dataset that carries none is refused
untouched.
Each also checks that every snapshot the record names is still the snapshot
it named, by guid, since a snapshot destroyed and taken again under the
same name is another snapshot.
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl -continue
Take the rebase on from the gate its record names, through the gates that
are left, in one process.
Applying is idempotent \(em every action means "make this true" \(em so what is
already true is left alone and what is not is made, which is why a fresh
run, a resumed one and a repair are one code path.
With
.Fl -verify
it prints, before each stage, how every action of the document stands, and
repairs the drift it finds on the clean actions; conflicted names are never
touched, because no action names one.
.It Fl -restart
Put the result back as onto was \(em the clone destroyed and made again from
the recorded onto snapshot with the same record, or the dataset rolled back
to its pre-apply snapshot \(em and apply the recorded manifest from the first
gate.
Nothing is decided again: the manifest is the decision.
A resolution's edits are discarded by definition.
.It Fl -abort
Release the holds, put the result back \(em the clone destroyed, or the
dataset rolled back to its pre-apply snapshot, that snapshot destroyed,
every one of its
.Sy zfs_rebase
properties taken off it and the dataset mounted where it belongs again \(em
destroy any snapshot the tool took for itself, unlink the manifest the
record names, and remove the run directory.
As if the run never happened.
.It Fl -verify
Alone on a result, report and write nothing at all, so that a deliberate
edit to a rebased file is shown and never overwritten.
Every action of the manifest, and of the resolution if there is one, is
classified as
.Sy done ,
.Sy pending
(the object is still as onto had it),
.Sy blocked
(impossible until a conflict beneath it is resolved),
.Sy drifted
(it matches neither the manifest's outcome nor onto's original) or
.Sy unchecked
(a tree it would have to be read against could not be found), and the
report gives each outcome's count and first name, plus a count of the names
outside the manifest that changed anyway.
.El
.Pp
Three test aids exist for the project's own harness and are not part of
ordinary use:
.Fl -posix
runs the whole decision over three plain directories given as
.Ar basedir fromdir ontodir
and writes the manifest, which is how the core is exercised where there is
no ZFS;
.Fl -build-fixture
materializes a fixture's
.Pa base ,
.Pa from ,
.Pa onto
and
.Pa expect
under a directory; and
.Fl -edit-fixture
turns a directory already holding one of those trees into another of them
by the smallest set of edits, so that everything the two agree on keeps its
inode and its ctime.
.Sh THE RECORD
The result carries the rebase as user properties, written by the create
itself in the clone form and set on the dataset before anything is touched
in the other.
User properties inherit down the naming tree, so every one of them is set
locally and read back as a local value only: an inherited value is no
record of ours.
.Bl -tag -width "zfs_rebase:manifest" -compact
.It Sy zfs_rebase:base
the branch point, with
.Sy :base_guid
.It Sy zfs_rebase:from
the side replayed, with
.Sy :from_guid
.It Sy zfs_rebase:onto
the side replayed onto, with
.Sy :onto_guid
.It Sy zfs_rebase:made
which inputs the tool snapshotted itself
.It Sy zfs_rebase:mode
strict or permissive
.It Sy zfs_rebase:form
clone or dataset
.It Sy zfs_rebase:tag
the tag its holds are filed under
.It Sy zfs_rebase:verify
whether
.Fl -verify
was asked for
.It Sy zfs_rebase:manifest
the absolute path the manifest was written to
.It Sy zfs_rebase:readonly
what
.Sy readonly
was before the run took the dataset over (dataset form only)
.It Sy zfs_rebase:state
the last gate the run passed
.El
.Pp
There is no state property at birth, and every verb keys on the record and
nothing else.
A dataset carrying neither
.Sy zfs_rebase:tag
nor
.Sy zfs_rebase:manifest
locally is not a result of this tool and is never touched: the verbs refuse
it and exit 2.
.Sh HOLDS
One persistent hold per input snapshot \(em base, from and onto, three of
them \(em is taken at the start of a real run, under a tag unique to the run:
.Sy zr-
and twelve hex digits, recorded as
.Sy zfs_rebase:tag .
The holds carry no cleanup descriptor, so they outlive the process, and
they are released only at done or at
.Fl -abort .
While they are there,
.Nm zfs Cm destroy
refuses each of the three with
.Dq dataset is busy
and
.Nm zfs Cm holds
shows the tag.
A stranded rebase holds on purpose: it is continuable.
In the clone form onto's snapshot cannot be destroyed for a second reason,
which is ZFS's own rule about a clone's origin and not something this tool
arranges.
.Sh THE GATES
A rebase's progress is a sequence of gates, written to
.Sy zfs_rebase:state
and nowhere else:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
applying1 -> conflicts -> applying2 -> done
applying1 -> done                          (no conflicts)
.Ed
.Pp
.Sy applying1
goes down immediately before the result stops being read-only, and the
clean actions of the manifest are applied under it whether the decision had
conflicts or not.
.Sy conflicts
is written after that apply verified, and is the hand-off to the conflict
manager, which leaves its answers in the run directory as
.Pa resolution ,
a manifest in the same format holding actions and no conflicts.
.Sy applying2
applies that file exactly as
.Sy applying1
applied the manifest.
.Sy done
is written after the result verified and is read-only again, and before the
holds are released, so that a kill in between leaves a record that says the
rebase finished and holds that
.Fl -abort
can still find.
.Pp
What a kill leaves is the last gate reached and nothing else.
There is no state at all until the first gate, and a stop writes none:
.Dv SIGINT ,
.Dv SIGTERM
and
.Dv SIGHUP
stop at the next action and put readonly back, leaving the state at the
gate;
.Dv SIGKILL
leaves the same gate with readonly off, which
.Fl -continue
restores.
.Fl -continue
resumes from the gate its record names, and does the same thing whichever
kind of stop left it:
.Bl -tag -width "applying2" -compact
.It no state
start at
.Sy applying1 :
applying nothing again is what an idempotent apply does over a tree nothing
was applied to
.It Sy applying1
apply the manifest again and go on
.It Sy conflicts
apply the resolution if it is there, else say what is being waited for and
exit 1
.It Sy applying2
apply the resolution again and go on
.It Sy done
do the cleanup a kill between the done gate and the release would have
skipped; with
.Fl -verify ,
repair
.El
.Sh FILES
Each run keeps its own directory, 0700 throughout, named for the dataset
carrying the record with its slashes kept as directories:
.Bl -tag -width "/var/db/zfs_rebase/<result>/resolution" -compact
.It Pa /var/db/zfs_rebase/<result>/mnt
where the result is mounted while the run has it
.It Pa /var/db/zfs_rebase/<result>/manifest
the manifest, unless
.Fl o
named somewhere else
.It Pa /var/db/zfs_rebase/<result>/resolution
where the conflict manager's answers are expected
.El
.Pp
Not
.Pa /var/run :
the system's cleanvar deletes every file there at boot, and a rebase
stopped at conflicts can outlast a reboot.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width "0" -compact
.It 0
the rebase is done
.It 1
it stopped at conflicts
.It 2
it was refused before anything was touched
.It 3
something failed part way
.El
.Pp
.Fl -verify
alone exits 0 when nothing is pending or drifted and 3 when something is;
blocked and unchecked are states and not faults.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Rebase a topic snapshot onto the current main, in the clone form:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zfs_rebase -v --from tank/topic@work --onto tank/main@current \e
    --result tank/rebased
.Ed
.Pp
The result is a read-only clone at
.Pa /var/db/zfs_rebase/tank/rebased/mnt .
The tool never promotes it: adopting it is yours to do by hand, when you
have looked at it and are satisfied.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zfs promote tank/rebased
zfs rename tank/main tank/main.old
zfs rename tank/rebased tank/main
zfs inherit mountpoint tank/main
zfs inherit readonly tank/main
.Ed
.Pp
The same rebase in the dataset form, which writes into
.Pa tank/main
itself and keeps
.Pa tank/main@pre
as the before-image:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zfs_rebase -v --from tank/topic --onto tank/main --result pre
.Ed
.Pp
A run that was killed part way is a rebase, not a leftover.
Look at the gate it left and take it on from there:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zfs get -s local -H -o value zfs_rebase:state tank/rebased
zfs_rebase --continue --result tank/rebased
.Ed
.Pp
Or take the whole thing away again:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zfs_rebase --abort --result tank/rebased
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr zfs 8 ,
.Xr zfs-hold 8 ,
.Xr zfs-promote 8 ,
.Xr zfs-rollback 8
.Sh CAVEATS
The tool never promotes.
The clone form's result is a read-only clone at a mountpoint of the run's
own choosing, and promoting, renaming or inheriting it is the user's work,
deliberately.
.Pp
Conflict resolution is not part of this tool.
A run with conflicts applies its clean actions, stops at the
.Sy conflicts
gate and waits for a resolution file to appear; reading the conflicts back,
presenting them and taking answers belong to a separate conflict manager.
.Pp
A verify after done is best effort.
Each input is looked for by name and then by guid across the pool, which is
what survives a rename or a promote, and what cannot be found is reported
as unchecked rather than guessed at.
Where a side was given as a dataset, the snapshot the tool took of it is
gone at done, so the actions that would have to read it cannot be checked
or redone afterwards.
After done, drift on a live dataset is indistinguishable from new work, so
a plain
.Fl -verify
before a
.Fl -continue Fl -verify
is the habit.
